          %h2#in_weak_foundations In weak foundations
          %p
            Many proofs rely explicitly on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation
            rule by first supposing that a polynomial function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            has no root and deriving a contradiction.
          %h3#in_constructive_mathematics In constructive mathematics
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+closure"} integral closure
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+closure"} algebraic closure
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            are not the same, because not every nonconstant polynomial function has a well-defined degree. This is still the case even if we take “nonconstant polynomial function”, we mean “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tight+apartness+relation"} apart from
            every constant polynomial function”. In general, every nonconstant polynomial function has a well-defined degree if and only if the field is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+field"} discrete field
          %p Thus, the fundamental theorem of algebra is usually expressed in terms of integral closure, which classically is the same as algebraic closure for any field:
          %ul
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+number"} complex number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrally+closed+field"} integrally closed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monic+polynomial"} monic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+function"} polynomial function
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/root"} root
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          %p
            There is a second problem, namely with Lemma
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#sqrt"}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto x^2 + c
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/root"} root
            for all complex numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in \mathbb{C}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+field"} Heyting field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+field"} discrete field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto x^2 + c
            has a root for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c = 0
            and for invertible
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in \mathbb{C}
            is either invertible or equal to zero. In fact, this fails in certain
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topoi"} topoi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            can only be proven in the presence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+countable+choice"} weak countable choice
            or the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+LPO"} analytic LPO
          %p
            However, the following weaker theorem is still true in constructive mathematics for the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulated+Cantor+real+number"} modulated Cantor complex numbers
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unramifiable+polynomial"} unramifiable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monic+polynomial"} monic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+function"} polynomial function
              with coefficients in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+root"} simple root
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          %p
            A fully choice-free constructive proof of this theorem by Wim Ruitenberg (
            %a{:href => "#Ruitenberg91"}> Ruitenberg 1991
            ) exists for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulated+Cantor+real+number"} modulated Cantor complex numbers
            (which agree with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+number"} Dedekind complex numbers
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+countable+choice"} weak countable choice
            or the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+LPO"} analytic LPO
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+number"} Dedekind complex numbers
            as well.
          %p
            In the absence of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi WCC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} WCC
            or the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+LPO"} analytic LPO
            %a{:href => "#Richman00"} Richman 2000
            has proposed that the FTA should be interpreted as a statement about sets of roots rather than about individual roots. He constructs a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+metric+space"} complete metric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{M}_n(\mathbb{C})
            which, classically, is the space of
            = succeed "-element" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisets"} multisets
            of complex numbers (and constructively is the completion of that space) and proves that every complex polynomial function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            may be associated with a point in this space in such a way that the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            elements of that point (when viewed as a multiset, if possible, and morally in any case) are the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            roots of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %h3#in_rca_0 In RCA_0
          %p
            Assuming classical logic, but weak foundations, it can be shown that FTA is true in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reverse+mathematics"} reverse mathematics
            system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi RCA
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RCA_0
            (
            %a{:href => "#TY2005"}> Tanaka-Yamazaki 2005
            ).
          %h2#History History
          %p
            The proof was attempted many times before Gauss gave what is accepted as the first proof in his dissertation (
            %a{:href => "#Gauss1799"}> Gauss 1799
            ), although this was not without issues (Gauss ‘fixed’ this proof almost 50 years later, but the last gap was not filled until the 20th century).
          %p
            All proofs of this fact (of which there are many) require something analytic, in the sense that ordinary algebra will not suffice: one needs to know that the real numbers (or the complex numbers) ‘have no algebraic gaps’. For instance, the rational numbers famously don’t contain the square root of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+closed+field"} real closed field
          %p
            However, it is of interest, partly theoretical, partly for the sake of finding the bare minimum needed to prove the FTA, to know an elementary proof, namely one that minimises the use of analytic techniques (for instance, the IVT for polynomial functions follows from the IVT for continuous functions, but that is like killing a mosquito with a bazooka). Gauss’ second proof (
            %a{:href => "#Gauss1866"}> Gauss 1866
            ) is elementary (and predates Artin’s by a long time). Since Gauss lacked modern algebraic techniques, some of his proof is laborious, but (
            %a{:href => "#Taylor85"}> Taylor 85
            ) gives a modern gloss. (With some amusing side notes: as Taylor puts it – ‘Gauss takes the opportunity [to] be rude to his inferior contemporaries’.) Gauss’ proof, in modern language, takes up less than a page and a half, but this presupposes familiarity with some of the theory of fields (but which is pure algebra). Artin’s proof, by comparison, drawing on major theorems can be given in half a page.
          %p It should be noted, in the context of the last statement, that proofs of the FTA can be given, relying on analytic ‘bazooka’ theorems, that are one sentence. However, to spell out the proofs of the necessary theorems, one needs a course in analysis, of some variety, so one is merely sweeping a lot under a very small rug.
          %h2#References References
          %ul
            %li#Gauss1799
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carl+Gauss"}> Carl Gauss
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Demonstratio nova theorematis functionem algebraicam rationalem integramunius variabilis in factores reales primi vel secundi gradus resolvi posse
                = succeed "))" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.jon-arny.com/httpdocs/Gauss/Gauss%20Fundamental%20Theorem.pdf"} English transl. pdf
            %li#Gauss1866
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carl+Gauss"}> Carl Gauss
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Demonstratio nova altera theorematis omnem functionem algebraicamrationalem integram unius variabilis in factores reales primi vel secundi gradus resolviposse
            %li
              %p
                Michael Eisermann.
                = succeed "." do
                  %em An Elementary Real-Algebraic Proof via Sturm Chains
                %a{:href => "http://www.jon-arny.com/httpdocs/Gauss/Constructive%20roots-annotated.pdf"} pdf
              %p
                %em Another new proof of the theorem that every integral rational algebraic function of one variable can be resolved into real factors of the first or second degree
                translated by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"} Paul Taylor
                and B. Leak (1983) (
                %a{:href => "http://www.paultaylor.eu/misc/gauss-web"}> web
                )
            %li#Taylor85
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"}> Paul Taylor
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gauss’ Second Proof
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.jon-arny.com/httpdocs/Gauss/Eureka-2-aug24.pdf"} pdf
          %p A proof of a variant of the fundamental theorem of algebra using finite multisets of complex numbers:
          %ul
            %li#Richman00
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Richman"}> Fred Richman
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The fundamental theorem of algebra: a constructive development without choice
              %strong 196
              1 (2000) 213–230 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.2140/pjm.2000.196.213"}> doi:10.2140/pjm.2000.196.213
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://msp.org/pjm/2000/196-1/pjm-v196-n1-p10-p.pdf"} pdf
          %p see also:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Richman"}> Fred Richman
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Constructive Mathematics without Choice
              %p
                in:
                %em Reuniting the Antipodes – Constructive and Nonstandard Views of the Continuum
                Synthese Library
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 306
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-015-9757-9_17"} doi:10.1007/978-94-015-9757-9_17
          %p The Reverse Mathematical treatment is given in
          %ul
            %li#TK2005
              Kazuyuki Tanaka and Takeshi Yamazaki,
              %em
                Manipulating the reals in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi RCA
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RCA_0
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %em Reverse Mathematics 2001
              %strong 21
              (2005)
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            algebraic proof of the fundamental theorem of algebra for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulated+Cauchy+real+numbers"} modulated Cauchy real numbers
            without choice princples such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+countable+choice"} weak countable choice
          %ul
            %li#Ruitenberg91
              Wim Ruitenberg, Constructing Roots of Polynomials over the Complex Numbers, Computational Aspects of Lie Group Representations and Related Topics, CWI Tract, Vol. 84, Centre for Mathematics and Computer Science, Amsterdam, 1991, pp. 107–128. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.mscsnet.mu.edu/~wim/publica/roots_new.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A full formalization in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rocq"} Rocq
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+assistant"} proof assistant
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              Herman Geuvers,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freek+Wiedijk"} Freek Wiedijk
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dl.acm.org/citation.cfm?id=696038"} web
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              MathOverflow,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/10535/ways-to-prove-the-fundamental-theorem-of-algebra"} Ways to prove the fundamental theorem of algebra
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  Despite the credit given to Gauss for his demonstration of 1799, Argand’s proof is often credited as the first one that is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em fully rigorous
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em geometrically
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℂ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
                  being also known as the Argand plane).
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
